---
// Selected work. Every claim here traces to master-profile.md in the resume workspace.
// Confidentiality: never name the employer, internal systems or timelines on the site.
import '../styles/diagram.css';
import Section from './Section.astro';
import CaseStudy from './CaseStudy.astro';
import Migration from './diagrams/Migration.astro';
import Pipeline from './diagrams/Pipeline.astro';
import Standards from './diagrams/Standards.astro';
---

<Section id="work" number="02" title="Selected work" meta="Professional work">
  <div class="cases">
    <CaseStudy
      number="01"
      kicker="Platform · Migration"
      title="Moving 20 microservices from EC2 to Amazon EKS"
      stats={[
        { value: '75%', label: 'lower compute cost after the move' },
        { value: '20', label: 'services containerised and migrated' },
        { value: '15', label: 'engineers on the team I supported through the move' },
      ]}
      tags={['Amazon EKS', 'Docker', 'Helm', 'Terraform', 'Spot + On-Demand', 'Cluster Autoscaler', 'HPA']}
      caption="Before and after: one service per server, then one cluster for all of them."
    >
      <p>
        The services ran one per EC2 instance, with only an early EKS proof of concept in place. I took ownership of the
        implementation: containerising every service, writing the Helm values, probes, storage and networking, and
        wiring up the CI/CD and integrations around them.
      </p>
      <p>
        I made the architecture calls on cluster layout, networking, deployment strategy and observability, and presented
        the target design to leadership. Cost came down through a mix of On-Demand and Spot node groups, rightsizing from real usage, and
        autoscaling with the Cluster Autoscaler and HPA. The work was recognised with an internal impact award.
      </p>
      <Migration slot="diagram" />
    </CaseStudy>

    <CaseStudy
      number="02"
      kicker="Delivery · Release engineering"
      title="One GitOps path from commit to production"
      stats={[
        { value: '5', label: 'environments on the same path: DEV, QA, UAT, PROD, DR' },
        { value: 'SHA', label: 'tagged images: every deploy traces back to a commit' },
      ]}
      tags={['GitLab CI', 'Argo CD', 'Helm', 'Amazon ECR', 'ServiceNow']}
      caption="CI builds and tags; Git records the change; Argo CD deploys it."
    >
      <p>
        I designed and maintain the GitLab CI/CD pipelines: a Maven build, a Docker image pushed to Amazon ECR, then an
        automatic update of the Helm values with that commit's tag. Argo CD notices the change and syncs it to the
        cluster, so what runs in each environment is always a commit you can read.
      </p>
      <p>
        I also run the human side of releases: deployment windows, approvals and change requests, post-deployment checks,
        rollbacks and hotfixes, across five environments including a DR setup I helped build.
      </p>
      <Pipeline slot="diagram" />
    </CaseStudy>

    <CaseStudy
      number="03"
      kicker="Platform · Engineering standards"
      title="Standards other teams build on"
      stats={[
        { value: '3', label: 'standards that teams beyond mine follow' },
      ]}
      tags={['GitLab CI', 'Terraform', 'Helm']}
      caption="Three standards, one baseline, many services."
    >
      <p>
        A good platform makes the right way the easy way. I wrote and maintain three standards that other teams follow:
        reusable GitLab CI templates, Terraform module conventions for naming, tagging and state layout, and a standard
        Helm chart with one values structure for every service.
      </p>
      <p>
        Pipelines stop drifting apart, infrastructure reads the same in every repository, and the tagging convention is
        what our cost reporting is built on.
      </p>
      <Standards slot="diagram" />
    </CaseStudy>
  </div>
</Section>
